Raise the search-thread stack from 8MB to 32MB.

Fairy-Stockfish pins every search thread's stack at TH_STACK_SIZE via
pthread_attr_setstacksize (active under USE_PTHREADS, which includes the
emscripten build; the wasm glue honors the attribute). Largeboard builds
carry ~70KB per recursive search frame (MAX_MOVES=8192 -> a 64KB ExtMove
buffer in each MovePicker), so a deep re-search line toward MAX_PLY needs
17-24MB and overflows the 8MB stack: natively a SIGSEGV / ASan
stack-overflow on the search thread, in wasm a silent overwrite of
adjacent linear memory that corrupts the instance before (or without)
trapping. Upstream: FSF issue #804; the structural fix is draft PR #1031
(per-thread MovePicker buffer pool) - adopt it when it lands and drop
this patch. Deterministic repro of record: engine/tests/stack-regress.cjs
(investigation 2026-08-27, evidence in engine/README.md).

Search-behavior-identical by construction: same tree, more room to walk
it. Applies to both pinned trees (the file is byte-identical); it is dead
code in the threadless ffish build, so only the engine artifact rebuilds.

diff --git a/src/thread_win32_osx.h b/src/thread_win32_osx.h
--- a/src/thread_win32_osx.h
+++ b/src/thread_win32_osx.h
@@ -26,14 +26,18 @@
 /// somewhat more than 1MB stack, so adjust it to TH_STACK_SIZE.
 /// The implementation calls pthread_create() with the stack size parameter
 /// equal to the linux 8MB default, on platforms that support it.
+/// Largeboard builds need far more: with MAX_MOVES=8192 a search frame
+/// carries a ~64KB MovePicker move buffer (~70KB per frame in total), so
+/// deep re-search lines toward MAX_PLY need 17-24MB of stack (FSF issue
+/// #804, measured 2026-08-27) - hence 32MB, one block per search thread.

 #if defined(__APPLE__) || defined(__MINGW32__) || defined(__MINGW64__) || defined(USE_PTHREADS)

 #include <pthread.h>

 namespace Stockfish {

-static const size_t TH_STACK_SIZE = 8 * 1024 * 1024;
+static const size_t TH_STACK_SIZE = 32 * 1024 * 1024;

 template <class T, class P = std::pair<T*, void(T::*)()>>
 void* start_routine(void* ptr)
